# If we are unsure, should we decide now or pay to learn?
next: {"title":"Is the QALY fair - and should the number decide?","href":"https://drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/dcast/health-technology-assessment-deciding/is-the-qaly-fair-and-should-the-number.cast"}

## The Price of Being Wrong
voice: male
level: advanced
use: distribution_curve
with:
    kind: normal
    mean_label: E[NMB] > 0
    show_sd_ticks: false
    shade:
      from: 0.75
      side: lower
      label: We adopt — and we were wrong
    title: 'Nusinersen: net monetary benefit across the PSA'

This series asks when a payer should decide now and when it should pay to learn, and it begins with nusinersen, whose probabilistic analysis returns not one net monetary benefit but this whole distribution.
    draw title axis curve mean_line

Expected NMB is positive at the severity-adjusted threshold, so the decision is adopt — but in this tail adoption destroys health, and the loss is the net benefit we gave up.
    draw shade shade_label

The expected value of perfect information is exactly that: the probability of being wrong times the size of the error, and nothing else — a study that resolves all uncertainty and confirms the decision you already took is worth precisely zero.
    highlight shade effect glow

Multiply that per-patient loss by everyone the decision covers, and you have the ceiling: no trial, however definitive, can be worth more than the cost of being wrong.
    text evpi_note "EVPI × N = ceiling on any study" #2f6b8f x 280 y 615 font_size 24

## Which parameter deserves a study?
voice: male
level: advanced
use: tornado_diagram
with:
    title: Which parameter is worth a study?
    x_label: Population EVPPI (million kr)
    base: 0
    show_values: true
    bars:
      - {label: Long-term survival, low: 0, high: 180}
      - {label: Motor-function utility, low: 0, high: 60}
      - {label: Drug price, low: 0, high: 0}

Now that we have seen what the whole of our uncertainty is worth, we can split that price by parameter: EVPPI asks what the nusinersen decision would gain if one input alone were resolved perfectly, the rest left exactly as uncertain as it is.
    draw axes base_line

Long-term survival on treatment dominates — around 180 million kroner of expected decision value, because it is the one parameter whose plausible range flips net monetary benefit across the threshold.
    draw bar_label_0 bar_0

Motor-function utility is worth a third of that, and the drug price is worth nothing at all to learn about, since it is not uncertain — it is negotiated.
    draw bar_label_1 bar_1 bar_label_2 bar_2

So the ranking is a research budget: a registry follow-up costing perhaps fifteen million clears a 180-million EVPPI comfortably, a utility study never could, and no single bar can exceed the overall EVPI that bounds them all.
    highlight bar_0 effect glow delivery grave

## Approve, reject, or buy information
voice: male
level: advanced
use: two_by_two_table
with:
    title: Approve, reject, or buy information
    row_label: Expected NMB at λ
    col_label: Population EVPI vs. cost of the study
    row_values:
      - NMB > 0
      - NMB < 0
    col_values:
      - EVPI > study cost
      - EVPI < study cost
    cells:
      - [Only in research, Approve]
      - [Reject + research, Reject]
    cell_notes:
      - ['managed entry, age limit', decide now]
      - [conditional on new data, no study changes it]
    highlight:
      - [0, 0]
    box:
      x: 60
      'y': 175
      w: 880
      h: 490

    mark mark_air target cell_0_0 kind box hidden true

Now that we have seen which single parameter deserves a study, notice that yes and no were never the only two things a committee could say.
    draw grid row_title col_title row_header_0 row_header_1 col_header_0 col_header_1

Down the right column the population EVPI is smaller than the study would cost — the residual uncertainty is cheaper than resolving it, so you decide now: approve above the threshold, reject below it.
    draw cell_0_1 cell_1_1

    pause 0.3

Down the left column the price of being wrong exceeds the price of finding out, and the decision is taken conditionally, with data still accruing.
    draw cell_0_0 cell_1_0

This top-left cell is approval only in research — every managed entry agreement, every price–volume deal, and nusinersen's age limit, which bounds the population exposed to the mistake while the evidence arrives.
    draw hl_0_0 mark_air title

And the quiet one is bottom right: a no that no study will ever overturn, which is the only honest place to stop asking for more data.
    highlight cell_1_1 effect glow

EVPI prices the mistake, EVPPI names the study, and this map decides whether you buy the information or take the risk — Beslutningsforum does not phrase it as arithmetic, but it is arithmetic all the same.
    delivery grave

